# Delivery contract

Rules constrain implementation. Skills run workflows. Reviewer subagents stay read-only.

For non-trivial work, do not implement until this sequence exists:

`request -> spec -> critique -> implementation plan -> implementation -> verification`

- Use `spec-driven-development`. Keep the spec in the repo (`docs/specs/<task-slug>.md` unless a local convention exists). Must-pass acceptance criteria are testable before coding.
- Send the spec to `spec-critic` before implementation. Send architecture-sensitive plans to `architecture-critic` before implementation.
- For meaningful UI/UX, use `frontend-design-quality`, add design acceptance criteria, and after a render exists send it to `design-critic`.
- After implementation, use `acceptance-verification` or `qa-verifier`. Do not claim completion until every must-pass criterion has evidence and no blocking reviewer finding remains.
- Reviewers report findings. The implementing agent fixes them and requests a fresh verification pass.
- If implementation reveals a new requirement or product decision, update the spec before coding that behavior. Code must not become the source of truth over the approved spec.

Skip this only for genuinely trivial edits: copy, typos, formatting, or an obvious isolated fix with no behavioral, contract, UX, data, architecture, dependency, or regression risk. When uncertain, write a compact spec.
